\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth,height=0.32\textheight,keepaspectratio]{fig4_attention_heatmap.pdf}
  \caption{Per-turn GAR-ours under AR generation ($N$\,=\,5 conversations, 10 turns). All fine-tuned variants cluster together (77--81\% decay; $\Delta$\,=\,3.3\,pp between ThreadGate and LoRA-only); Base decays 87.5\%. Gating adds zero incremental effect on the attention decay pattern.}
  \label{fig:attention-heatmap}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Inference-Time Attention Steering}\label{subsec:steering}

All training-time interventions leave the exposure bias gap intact. If the collapse is caused by attention misallocation rather than representation degradation, then directly correcting attention should recover GAR. We test this by adding a constant bias $\beta$ to pre-softmax attention logits for goal-token positions during generation:
\begin{equation}
  \tilde{A}_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} = A_{i,j}^{(\ell,h)} + \beta \cdot \mathbf{1}[j \in \mathcal{G}],
\end{equation}
where $\tilde{A}$ denotes the modified logits before softmax. The additive form operates in log-probability space, providing uniform boost even when baseline attention is near zero. The response is sharply nonlinear: $\beta \in [0, 2]$ yields marginal improvement, then $\beta = 3$ triggers a phase transition to GAR\,=\,0.556 (9\% PPL cost). At $\beta$\,=\,5, GAR reaches 0.727$\pm$0.061 (PPL 1.77); the operating region $\beta \in [3, 5]$ exceeds the TF baseline while PPL remains below 1.8 (Figure~\ref{fig:steering-recovery}; full results in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering}; per-$\beta$ turn-level profiles in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-steering-viz}).

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_attention_steering.pdf}
  \caption{Inference-time $\beta$-steering recovers GAR from 0.064 to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation as the proximal cause (9\% PPL cost at $\beta$\,=\,3; phase transition at $\beta \in [2, 3]$).}
  \label{fig:steering-recovery}
\end{figure}

This closes the causal loop: if the collapse were due to representation drift in hidden states, no amount of attention reweighting could recover GAR. The recovery confirms attention misallocation, not representation degradation, as the proximal cause (qualitative examples in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-examples}).

\subsection{Scheduled Sampling Compresses the TF--AR Gap}\label{subsec:ss}

Scheduled sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, which mixes model-generated tokens into training prefixes with linearly increasing probability (implementation details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-ss}), directly targets the training-inference mismatch identified in \S\ref{subsec:dist-shift}. Across three seeds, AR GAR-ours rises from 0.064 to $0.401\pm0.047$ (seeds: 0.347 / 0.423 / 0.433; per-seed details in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-perseed}), compressing the gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (per-seed gaps: 1.45$\times$, 1.17$\times$, 1.15$\times$; TF GAR $0.498\pm0.004$) with no PPL cost ($1.441\pm0.001$; Figure~\ref{fig:ss-gap}). Steering on SS models is remarkably stable ($\beta$\,=\,5: $0.717\pm0.001$): the training-time seed variance (std\,=\,0.047 at $\beta$\,=\,0) is nearly eliminated (Figure~\ref{fig:ss-multiseed}), suggesting $\beta$-steering converges to a seed-independent attention equilibrium. The improvement generalizes across GAR definitions: SS raises mid-turn GAR above 0.36 under all four alternative token-position variants (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-gar-robust}), while ThreadGate only improves last-token GAR (0.325 vs.\ all-token 0.025): SS builds a broad goal-attention pattern, not position-specific overfitting. GAR-Dongre also rises substantially under SS ($N$\,=\,5 pilot; see Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), the only intervention that moves both metrics. The benefit generalizes across architectures: on Llama-3.1-8B, SS compresses the gap from ${\sim}$12$\times$ to 2.9$\times$ (AR GAR 0.166, TF GAR 0.477), weaker than Qwen's ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ likely due to fewer training epochs (2 vs.\ 3; \S\ref{subsec:cross-model}). The benefit diminishes in longer dialogues (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix-n5}), consistent with exposure bias compounding over turns.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_gap_compression.pdf}
  \caption{Scheduled sampling compresses the TF-AR gap from 7.8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$, the only variant approaching parity across five training conditions.}
  \label{fig:ss-gap}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_ss_multiseed.pdf}
  \caption{Multi-seed AR GAR under scheduled sampling ($\beta$\,=\,0 and $\beta$\,=\,5, three seeds). Steering nearly eliminates seed variance (std: 0.047$\to$0.001).}
  \label{fig:ss-multiseed}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Teacher-forced evaluation.} TF GAR-ours is 0.484 (TG) and 0.481 (LoRA-only), confirming that gating provides zero benefit under teacher forcing on Llama, consistent with the Qwen results (0.50 vs.\ 0.50).

\paragraph{Autoregressive evaluation.} AR evaluation uses $N$\,=\,20 mid-turn conversations (turns 3--$T$, excluding the first two turns to avoid initialization effects). The AR GAR distribution is heavy-tailed: the coefficient of variation exceeds 100\% for ThreadGate (mean 0.043, std 0.100), and 5\% of turn-level measurements exhibit sporadic attention spikes (GAR $\geq$ 0.4). We therefore report both mean and median.

\paragraph{Gate profile.} Llama's gate profile differs qualitatively from Qwen's: Qwen shows minimum attenuation at early layers (L1\,=\,0.793, L5\,=\,0.797), while Llama shows a V-shaped valley at mid-to-late layers (L25\,=\,0.719, L24\,=\,0.809). Despite this architectural difference, both models converge toward identity under NTP pressure, and the TF-AR gap is comparable (27$\times$ by median for Llama vs.\ 8--17$\times$ for Qwen).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering\small
\caption{Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct complete results. TF: teacher-forced ($N$\,=\,50); AR: autoregressive ($N$\,=\,20, mid-turn). Median is more representative due to heavy-tailed AR distributions.}
\label{tab:llama-full}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{TF}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{AR}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
& GAR-O & PPL & GAR-O (mean) & GAR-O (med) \\
\midrule
Base & 0.017 & 3.45 & 0.009 & 0.009 \\
ThreadGate & 0.484 & 2.57 & 0.043 & 0.018 \\
LoRA-only & 0.481 & 2.60 & 0.028 & 0.018 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_crossmodel.pdf}
  \caption{Cross-architecture validation. (a)~The TF-AR gap persists on Llama-3.1-8B (27$\times$ by median), exceeding Qwen's 8$\times$. (b)~Gate profiles on normalized depth: Qwen dips at early layers, Llama at late layers; architecture-dependent patterns, but both converge toward identity.}
  \label{fig:crossmodel}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Cross-model summary.} Table~\ref{tab:crossmodel-detail} consolidates the cross-model comparison, showing TF GAR, AR GAR, PPL, and gap ratios side by side for both architectures. The consistent pattern---TF GAR $\approx$ 0.5, AR GAR $\ll$ 0.1, gap $\geq$ 7.9$\times$---across two architectures with different layer counts, attention head configurations, and pre-training corpora provides strong evidence that exposure bias is architecture-independent. Notably, Llama's larger gaps (11.2--17.2$\times$ vs.\ 7.9--13.5$\times$) may reflect its deeper architecture (32 vs.\ 28 layers), which allows more layers for distributional drift to compound through.

\input{../artifacts/appendix_expansion/tab_crossmodel_detail.tex}

\paragraph{GAR-Dongre cross-model comparison.} GAR-Dongre shows negligible distribution shift on Llama (AR/TF ratio 1.002$\times$), compared to 1.28$\times$ on Qwen. This confirms the mechanism specificity finding: exposure bias selectively disrupts the concentrated last-token attention pattern (GAR-ours) while leaving the diffuse all-token attention (GAR-Dongre) unaffected across both architectures.

\paragraph{Training details.} Llama variants used the same pipeline as Qwen: 8K UltraChat conversations, LoRA rank 16 targeting $q$/$v$ projections, 3 epochs. Training loss converged to 0.929 (LoRA-only) and 0.934 (ThreadGate); the slight difference is attributable to the additional GAR loss component. Wall-clock time was 7.0h (LoRA-only) and 7.3h (ThreadGate), approximately 17\% longer than Qwen due to Llama's larger layer count (32 vs.\ 28 layers).

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Attention to system-prompt goals decays 70.8\% over five conversation turns on Qwen-2.5-7B-Instruct, despite residual stream probes confirming that goal representations persist (AUC\,=\,0.98; \citealp{dongre2026attention}). The decay is not model-specific: \citet{dongre2026attention} documented 27--48\% Goal Accessibility Ratio (GAR) decline across 12 models, and it has concrete downstream consequences: instruction compliance drops by up to 24 percentage points on content-constrained tasks (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

Existing interventions operate at inference time or are turn-agnostic. Split-softmax \citep{li2024measuring} separates instruction from conversation tokens; StreamingLLM \citep{xiao2024streaming} retains anchor tokens; Gated Attention \citep{qiu2025gated} applies per-head sigmoid gates within a single forward pass. None addresses the training--inference mismatch at the source. Auxiliary attention losses, which optimize GAR alongside next-token prediction (NTP), offer a training-time alternative, but whether such objectives transfer from teacher-forced training to autoregressive generation has not been tested. Classical exposure bias mitigation \citep{bengio2015scheduled,lamb2016professor} targets RNN sequence generation; no multi-turn transformer equivalent exists.

We introduce ThreadGate, per-layer content-and-turn-conditioned MLP gates on the residual stream ($x_{l+1} = x_l + g_l \cdot f_l(x_l)$), not as a remedy but as a controlled testbed for isolating failure mechanisms. A five-way comparison (ThreadGate, +PCGrad, Uniform Gate, LoRA-only, Base) forms a causal exclusion chain (\S\ref{sec:comparison-design}): each adjacent pair kills one alternative hypothesis, tracing the failure to exposure bias \citep{bengio2015scheduled}, ruling out gradient conflict, gating architecture, and turn conditioning.

\begin{figure*}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\textwidth,height=0.32\textheight,keepaspectratio]{fig1_headline.pdf}
  \caption{Training succeeds but inference collapses. Under TF evaluation, all fine-tuned variants converge to GAR-ours$\approx$0.50; under AR generation ($N$\,=\,50), GAR collapses 7.8--16.7$\times$. Log scale; dashed slopes connect paired TF-AR values.}
  \label{fig:headline}
\end{figure*}

Training succeeds: all variants converge to TF GAR\,=\,0.50 (Figure~\ref{fig:headline}). Under autoregressive generation, GAR collapses to 0.019--0.064 (8--17$\times$). PCGrad eliminates gradient conflict but \emph{worsens} AR GAR ($p$\,=\,0.002), ruling it out by \emph{modus tollens}. Steering ($\beta$\,=\,5) recovers GAR to 0.727; scheduled sampling compresses the gap to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ (\S\ref{subsec:ss}). Our contributions: (1)~first empirical measurement of exposure bias on auxiliary attention losses (8--27$\times$ across two architectures); (2)~PCGrad-based causal exclusion of gradient conflict ($p$\,=\,0.002, effect reverses); (3)~inference-time $\beta$-steering recovers GAR to 0.727, confirming attention misallocation rather than representation degradation as the proximal cause; (4)~cross-architecture validation on Llama-3.1-8B; (5)~first evidence that time-scheduled sampling \citep{bengio2015scheduled} transfers to auxiliary attention objectives in multi-turn transformers, compressing the gap from 8$\times$ to ${\sim}$1.3$\times$ ($0.401\pm0.047$, three seeds). Code and data are available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/turn-residual-gating}.

